Q: How to fix the position with html and css flexbox and position? A: <p>The issue is that your two .horizontal divs and the &lt;p&gt; are direct children of .contenu-central, so they stack according to that container’s layout instead of sitting on one row. Don’t try to fix this with position: absolute — wrap the three elements in their own flex row and let flexbox do the alignment:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;contenu-central&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;divider&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;horizontal&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;ΔΑΙΜΩΝ · TARTARUS&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;horizontal&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;title-block&quot;&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;red &lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;demon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: In outer transaction method, 1st inner DB write via Oracle SP not rolled back after exception in 2nd inner one A: <p>Your outer <code>@Transactional</code> is never applied, because the <code>performDBWrites</code> is called directly from <code>executeTask()</code> as method in the same class, and <code>@Transactional</code> only works when the call goes through the proxy - same self-invocation rule you already know, just applied to the outer method.</p>
<p>To fix it, move <code>performDBWrites</code> into a separate bean and call it through an injected reference:</p>
<pre><code>  @Service
  public class DBWriteService {
      @Autowired 
      ResponseService responseService;

      @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
      public void performDBWrites(String refId) throws Exception { ... }
  }

  @Component
  public class Job {
      @Autowired 
      DBWriteService dbWriteService;   // proxy

      @Scheduled(cron = &quot;...&quot;)
      public void executeTask() {
          try { 
              dbWriteService.performDBWrites(refId);
          }
          catch (Exception e) { 
              log.error(&quot;Error&quot;, e);
          }
      }
  }
</code></pre>
